"/_next/static/chunks/3ppkpjc789k8m.js\\\"],\\\"Image\\\"]\\n21:T154d,\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"Shri Ashapuran Parshwanath. Parshwanath 70 of 108 - Shri Ashapuran Parshwanath.. Place: Noon, Taluka: Sirohi, District: Sirohi.. Main Deity: An idol of Lord Shri Ashapuran Parshwanath in white stone, in the padmasana posture, 17 inches tall and 12.5 inches wide. Above the head of the idol is a canopy of 7 serpent hoods. It is believed that after worshipping this idol of Shri Ashapuran Parshwanath, a person's seven kinds of fear disappear from their life.. Location: This is in Noon village, Sirohi district.. History: This is one of many splendid, forgotten temples of the past. This now-deserted village of Rajasthan was once a prosperous town in ancient times. It is believed that this temple was built by the Jain Sangh around Vikram Samvat 1100. During the period of the town's prosperity, it was renovated by many people. Other neighboring villages have now become deserted as well. There is no Jain population living here at present. It is believed that if worshipped with great faith, the wishes of devotees are fulfilled here - and this continues to hold true even today. Hence the idol in the Noon temple is called \\\"Ashapuran Parshwanath\\\" (fulfiller of hopes). A fair is held here every year on the 10th day of the dark half of Paush.. Other Temples: There is no other temple.. Art and Sculpture: This temple is very ancient. This ancient and once-famous tirtha of the past has been forgotten. If fairs were organized and arrangements made for pilgrims at these unknown yet powerful tirthas, this tirtha could be brought to light and made famous. At present, this temple is administered by the Kalindri Sangh.. Directions: The nearest railway station to the temple, Sirohi Road, is 56 km away. Bus service and private vehicles are available. There are no boarding or lodging facilities. This tirtha is 20 km from Mandoli, 29 km from Jalore, and 38 km from Bhinmal. Since there is no Jain population here, the Shri Kalindra Jain Sangh manages this tirtha. This tirtha is definitely worth visiting.. Scripture References: Ashapuran Parshwanath is mentioned in \\\"365 Shri Parshwajin Naammala.\\\" A beautiful metal idol of Ashapuran Parshwanath is installed as the Main Deity on the first floor of the temple at Ramnagar, Sabarmati, Gujarat.. Stuti: \\\"Though free of desire like an ascetic, still you fulfill all hopes; you who bore the afflictions (upasarga) also dispel all troubles. O mother of the world, dwelling in the town of Noon, there is no deficiency (nyunta) in you. 'To Shri Ashapuran Parshwa, with devotion, I offer my vandana.'\\\". Chanting Mantra: \\\"Om Hreem Arham Shri Ashapuran Parshwanathaya Namah\\\". Trust: Shri Ashapuran Parshwanath Shwetambar Jain Tirth, Post: Noon, Via: Kalindri, District: Sirohi, State: Rajasthan, India.. Purushadaniya Arhat Parshwanath Charitra, Part 12 (Lord Parsha's Varshidan, the year-long charity): Even earlier, in his life as a householder, the wondrous Parshwanath had attained transcendent, indestructible avadhi jnana (clairvoyant knowledge) and avadhi darshan (clairvoyant perception). Along with this, Parsha Kumar felt that the proper time for renunciation (abhinishkraman) had come. Knowing this, he renounced diamonds, gold, wealth, and kingdom, along with the kingdom's vehicles, treasury and riches, cities, inner palaces, provinces and so forth; he gave up boundless wealth, gold, jewels, pearls, conch shells, thrones and coral. He gave away his remaining necessary possessions, such as rubies and the like, to his self-appointed attendants.. Purushadaniya Arhat Parshwanath Charitra, Part 13 (The procession of Lord Parsha's diksha, or renunciation): Arhat Parshwanath was seen by a thousand eyes, praised by a thousand mouths, and saluted by a thousand hearts. Having seen the Lord, people began to form a thousand aspirations. Seeing the Lord's beautiful radiance and form, people longed for that same radiance and form; he was a sight for a thousand eyes. The Lord, with his right hand, accepted the salutations of thousands of men and women, passing through rows of thousands of homes, hearing the sweet and beautiful sounds of the jaynaad (cries of victory) accompanied by veena, hand-cymbals, instruments, songs and music; adorned with garments and ornaments carrying canopies and chamaras (fly-whisks) held aloft; escorted by soldiers mounted on elephants, horses, mules and palanquins; amid all kinds of performers and all kinds of musical instruments; through every quarter of the inner city, amid flowers, fragrances, garlands and ornaments of every kind, with instruments of every kind, with such great wealth, immense talent, a vast army, a vast retinue of vehicles, a vast populace, and all together - army, elephants, horses, camels, mules, palanquins, litters, and every kind of vehicle - all with complete propriety, complete honor, that is, with the full desire of their wealth, all adorned with the finest ornaments - merchants, kshatriyas, the eighteen varnas and all - with all kinds of performers, cymbal players, fragrant flowers, garlands, and the display of ornaments, and skill in playing musical instruments, with such great wealth, immense talent, a vast army, vast vehicles, a vast populace, all together enhancing the glory of Parshwanath's grand procession. To the resounding of conches, earthen drums, bheri, jhalar, kharmukhi, hudukka, dundubhi and other instruments, the diksha procession of Purushadaniya Arhat Parshwanath Prabhu shone forth in splendor.\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1b:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mb-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"a\\\",null,{\\\"href\\\":\\\"https://goo.gl/maps/qqtJBbpcfB6cYuAQA\\\",\\\"target\\\":\\\"_blank\\\",\\\"rel\\\":\\\"noopener noreferrer\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-sm bg-maroon px-5 py-2.5 font-u